  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA powerful eastern CONUS upper level trough is anchoring a \ncontinental polar air airmass over the forecast area. This will \ncontinue to support light freeze/frost threats this weekend over\nmainly northern/eastern portions of the area, where surface high\npressure will have the most influence. The upper trough will \nshift east Sunday, and this will allow a quick-moving shortwave \ntrough in the Pacific stream to move across our region. Given how\ndry the air is preceding the system, any rainfall will be light\nand mostly confined to northeast Louisiana and southwest\nMississippi. \n\nLooking beyond the early week system and through next weekend, \nstrong deep layer high pressure will become a fixture over the \nsoutheast CONUS and eastern seaboard region. This is a classic dry\nfall weather pattern, and unfortunately, it offers little hope \nfor any rainfall. Daily temperature ranges will be quite large \n(up to 35 to 40 deg F) in the dry and well mixed airmass. /EC/",
